# Inventory service

A small HTTP service that tracks stock levels for the warehouse team.

## Commands

- Install: `npm ci`
- Lint: `npm run lint`
- Test: `npm test`
- Build: `npm run build`

## Project structure

```
src/
  index.js        entry point, starts the server
  routes/
    items.js      CRUD for items
    stock.js      stock movements
  db/
    pool.js       database connection pool
    migrations/   SQL migrations, applied in order
  lib/
    money.js      price helpers
test/
  items.spec.js
  stock.spec.js
```

## Architecture

The server is a plain Node HTTP server with a tiny router in `src/index.js`.
Each route module exports a handler map keyed by method.
The database layer is a thin wrapper over a connection pool.

## Gotchas

- Prices are stored in cents as integers; never use floats for money.
- `npm test` needs `DATABASE_URL` pointing at a scratch database.
- Migrations are not reversible; write a new one instead of editing an old one.
- The stock endpoint is called by the scanner devices every second; keep it fast.

## Decisions

- We chose plain SQL over an ORM to keep queries visible in review.
- Stock movements are append-only; the current level is a sum, never an update.
- Item ids are UUIDs generated by the database, not by the client.

## Style

- Two-space indentation, single quotes, semicolons.
- Keep functions short; extract a helper when a handler grows past a screen.

